{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low-bias: model systematically underpredicts realized prices during the uptrend (often by ~1–4 EUR), especially from ~day 5 to ~day 25. Apply an upward level shift to the whole path when recent history shows rising momentum.",
    "Trend/lag error: forecasts react too slowly to regime changes (miss the sustained rally). If the last 5–10 days show higher highs/higher lows, increase the slope of the forecast (add a positive drift that grows with horizon).",
    "Horizon-specific bias: short horizon (days 1–3) errors are smaller; mid-horizon (days ~7–20) underprediction is largest. Use a ramp correction: small uplift near-term, larger uplift mid-horizon, then taper.",
    "Over-mean-reversion at long horizon: model often pulls prices back toward mid-60s while outcomes stay elevated (high-60s). Reduce mean-reversion strength when spot is above its 20–30d average and the average is rising.",
    "Spike handling: when model predicts sharp late spikes (e.g., jumps to 70+), it tends to overshoot and/or mis-time them versus outcomes. Damp late-horizon spikes and spread the move earlier (smooth peaks; cap single-day jumps).",
    "Turning-point asymmetry: model is better at calling pullbacks than sustained rallies; when volatility rises and prices break above recent range, favor continuation over reversion (increase continuation weight after upside breakouts)."
  ]
}
